aditionally Zojiq, Zoiq, was changed over to Zojz during Albanisation of Kosovar toponyms. Attested as Ottoman Turkish زویك (zoyik) in 1591, and as Serbo-Croatian Zojić /Зојић.","tpl":[{"k":29,"lang":61,"form":"زویك"},{"k":29,"lang":"sh","form":"Zojić//Зојић"}]}},{"w":"Zojz","p":"PROPN","s":[{"g":"alternative form of Zojs","t":179}],"f":[["Zojzi",1]]},{"w":"Zojz","p":"PROPN","s":[{"g":"pagan god of lightning","t":183,"x":["Zoizi ašt ńi katunn n Zadrim. Por n ḱoft se θon atü katunnart „Paša Zoizin!“, nuk kan n menn katunnin, por ńi zot.","Por kü zot, tśi i θon Zoiz, ište i pari i zotave. Ket aδrošin mą fort. Kü Zoizi kište er̄fén n dor e mennošin džinńa e heršem, se ašt ni hüiní e rebt, se rin ner rę́ me dźiθ er̄fé n dor."]}],"f":[["Zojzi",1]],"ety":{"text":"First attested in a folk tale recorded by Maximilian Lambertz, published in 1922, by an informer from Zadrimë who aside from describing Zojz as the god of lightning, also told how the god's son was called Pluton, god of the underworld, who made a hole in his father's head from which Prende came out, goddess of wisdom, matching to perfection the myth of the birth of Athena. The informer also called the god surely Illyrian, and he was also familiar with the town of Zojz in Zadrimë, which he considered evidence for the existence of the god. Parts of his tale were then reported in many following…","tpl":[{"k":29,"lang":38,"form":"*dyḗws"}]}}],"zoëi":[{"w":"Zoëi","p":"PROPN","s":[{"g":"a female given name","t":2}]}],"çarë":[{"w":"çarë","p":"ADJ","s":[{"g":"cleaved, cut, split"}],"f":[["i çarë",29],["të çara",92],["çam",0],["çamun",0],["çamën",0]],"ipa":[{"i":"/ˈt͡ʃaɾə/"}],"ety":{"text":"Participle form of çaj.","tpl":[]}},{"w":"çarë","p":"NOUN","s":[{"g":"tear, rift, crack"}],"f":[["e çarë",28],["të çara",3],["e çara",1],["të çarat",5],["çam",0],["çamun",0],["çamën",0]],"ipa":[{"i":"/ˈt͡ʃaɾə/"}],"ety":{"text":"Participle form of çaj.","tpl":[]}}]}}
